Mission Control Architecture	OpenClaw: Local-first, single Node.js Gateway process with file-based memory (Markdown/SQLite) and serial execution [1]. SiteGPT: 14 agents on a single VPS using a 15-minute manual polling loop [1].	Event-driven architecture using NATS JetStream for sub-millisecond reactive swarming [1]. Employs staggered cron jobs where each tick creates an isolated one-shot session to query a shared Convex database [2, 3].	Mission Control actually works via simplified staggered cron schedules and a shared Convex activity feed rather than the complex real-time push protocols people assume [2, 3].	Reduces monthly costs from $50,000 to $500 by implementing RouteLLM for model routing (80-90% to cheap models) and Anthropic/OpenAI prompt caching (90% savings) [2, 3].	Enforces the 'Rule of Two' (agents satisfy max 2 of 3: private data, untrusted content, external comms) and utilizes WASM or Firecracker sandboxing for non-negotiable tool isolation [2-4].	Phase 0 - 'Hello World': Ship exactly 3 hardcoded agents (Researcher, Analyst, Writer) doing one task to reach time-to-first-value in <5 minutes [2, 3].	[1-3]
Consensus & Decision Protocols	OpenClaw: Single-user gateway with basic session routing [5]. SiteGPT: Lead agent 'Jarvis' acts as chief orchestrator and quality manager [6].	Confidence-weighted voting using diverse model architectures (final_answer = argmax(∑(confidence×vote))) [2, 3].	ACL 2025 research indicates Voting (with 'All-Agents Drafting') outperforms Debate for accuracy; discussion rounds can reduce performance via artificial convergence [2, 3].	Voting protocols improve reasoning tasks by 13.2%; Collective Improvement (CI) refinement adds +7.4% [2, 3].	Governance-as-a-Service (GaaS) with Trust Factors [1] and coercive/normative enforcement modes to treat agents as untrusted by default [2, 3].	Phase 1 - 'Configurable Crew': Implementing LangGraph orchestration and basic NATS messaging visible in the dashboard [2, 3].	[2, 3, 5, 6]